ally ill-advised  yes so step one is you need a cfo on a  cfo that actually knows how to take you  public  a lot of people give you advice that you  should hire a cfo that has taken a  company public  that is hard for many reasons  for one there's just not very many such  people  secondly  cfos turn out to be very highly paid  individuals  and when you go public you get paid a  lot as a cfo so what ends up happening  is a lot of these people who are cfos  who've taken a company public are just  very rich  and they don't want to work for you  anymore they're just like whatever i  have my house in the hamptons and that's  this is where i am and i'm just not  going to go work with you  so it's just a very limited pool of  people who have taken a company public  once  and so  there's that and then we did interview  some some people and you know they had  good experience what we ended up doing  for cfo is not take somebody who had  taken a company public but you know he  obviously knew finance etc he came from  goldman sachs but we maximized for  kind of  intelligence and grit  and and that was a really good move for  us and so that that worked out for us  quick question so intelligence i can see  how you test for intelligence how do you  assess grit ah it's just you know i i  think a lot of it has to do from  references i mean this one  matt that we hired we we knew a few  people that knew him  and you know every there's a reference  we're just and they were true the thing  is they were truly they'll tell you like  oh matt garupa is like  the hardest working most  and also most honest guy i've ever met  and i'm like to me the words honest and  cfo just kind of don't go together that  much i don't i don't know if i just had  i was biased against that but then we  hired a guy that is like the most honest  guy like we call him  sometimes some of us call him the boy  scout because it's just like this guy is  just kind of a good guy  and so we hired a mat  and you know by the way the other thing  with matt is matt was going to be based  in new york right before covent we hired  matt  i don't know his first day was maybe a  month before covet before like it's  probably like february of 2020 or  something like that he was gonna be  based in new york but we're  headquartered in pittsburgh we have an  office in new york with about 100 people  he was going to be in that office but he  knew he had to travel out to pittsburgh  on his first day he shows up with a  i don't know it had like 10 slides a  deck with his travel plans for the next  two years  he had  pie charts a fraction of time he was  going to spend like in pittsburgh in new  york on the plane like it's all this  stuff that he had done like a massive  analysis like what a consultant would do  for some sort of analysis he's like this  is my travel schedule for the next two  years and i'm like  wow i i would have never done anything  like this but i see we hired the right  person  he must have spent hours and hours on  this and then poor guy kovit came and  then that travel plan went down the  drain and um he just didn't have to  travel  but anyways you hire cfo that's kind of  the first thing and then  and then  for me i think what really  mattered was  is our revenue and this seems to be like  what matters the most is our revenue  predictable is our finances predictable  if your finances are not predictable you  should not go public you're going to  have a real bad time  so you know you want to know that your  finances are predictable and by  predictable i mean  i've learned  you need to be able to predict your  finances with you know a year out  and be like three percent away  so this is a pretty good prediction that  you have to pretty good forecasting so  you have to do that  and you have to have a path to real  profitability that's the other thing  if you don't have either of those i  would say  i i would not go public uh you're just  having a really bad time  the other thing 